**Read:** [Modules in Mechanics of Materials — David Roylance](../sources/Mechanics-of-Materials-Roylance.lingua#r_7K2RYA9504FGFGJTVWHMWXD5WY), Trusses and Beam Displacements; [Applied Mechanics of Solids — Allan F. Bower](../sources/Applied-Mechanics-of-Solids-Bower.lingua#r_109WFZBWMEMGHV9TGFD22H2F1W), energy methods in chapter 5 and the introduction to finite elements in chapter 7.

Equilibrium alone may leave more unknown reactions than independent equations. Material laws and displacement compatibility provide the remaining relationships. Adding a support can reduce displacement but create thermal and assembly loads that did not exist in a statically determinate version.

For a bar restrained at both ends, an imposed free expansion is balanced by elastic compression. For a continuous beam, moments depend on relative span stiffness and support settlements. An ideal constraint is a model of finite hardware: a contact can open, a support can slide, and a joint can have compliance.

**Work:** Solve a redundant bar or beam by releasing one reaction, calculating the displacement caused by loads, then restoring the reaction required by compatibility. Solve the same problem with assembled stiffness equations. Include a prescribed support movement and a changed modulus. Check the limit as the extra support becomes very soft, then identify a case in which treating it as infinitely stiff gives a poor load prediction.

